tarted ten years ago  when mullah Mohammad Omar was  in charge of the Taliban but now finally  after the office of the Taliban is open  in 2003 2013 rather in Doha they have  inked this agreement according to this  agreement all sides will have to abide  by their respective terms the United  States will gradually withdraw its  troops you see the 7-day reduction in  violence expanded to 135 days and then a  graduate of withdrawal within 14 months  of all American and foreign troops they  will be initially leaving five bases as  well the Afghan government is going to  push the United Nations to remove  members of the Taliban from the  sanctions list the Afghan Taliban is  going to not just reduce violence but it  is going to continue its fight against  ISIL and make sure that its soil is  never used again by al Qaeda  so there's a lot at stake but again a  first step towards lasting peace in  Afghanistan has been taken you heard  from the Qatari foreign minister as well  that the state of Qatar has played an  important role at a time when people had  forgotten that there is still a chance  for peace in Afghanistan and it took up  the mantle and has been facilitating  that process you also heard from the  Pakistani Foreign Minister earlier  saying that Pakistan remains committed  welcomes a prosperous and peaceful  Afghanistan for its neighbors as well  and on that note of whether Pakistan's  dealings with the Taliban are going to  be monitored by the United States he  told us that Pakistan has nothing to  hide and Pakistan will be making sure  that all its dealings are in the open so  it is a historic and momentous occasion  but like you said the proof is going to  be in the pudding that in the coming  days and months are going to decide  where the peace in Afghanistan is going  to move from a signed document to  reality on the ground there are many you  might say roadblocks in the way as well  as are makers for example we have a  fractious government in Kabul at the 