y  progresses let's cross over to the US  now our correspondent Alan Fisher is  standing by in Washington DC this is a  huge foreign policy success for  President Trump he is obviously going to  run with this in what is an important  year for him big tick in the box and he  needed a foreign policy success you  remember that he essentially put all his  eggs in the North Korean basket in the  first two years how the two leaders  there had written wonderful letters to  one another Donald Trump saying that  they fell in love here he's promised  that he will start to withdraw American  troops and and that certainly seen  to be the Gaul here at the moment  there's around twelve thousand there  Donald Trump has been speaking for years  about taking troops out of Afghanistan  he believes that while it was right to  go into Afghanistan that they have been  there too long and that is certainly a  view shared by many Americans and not  just Trump supporters  if these interlocking agreements as Mike  Pompeo describes them works then the  United States will start withdrawing  troops within the next hundred days or  so down from 12,000 to about 8,000 600  and that number will continue to  decrease now put that in perspective  during the surges that we saw in  Afghanistan when the United States was  seeking a military victory there was  about 120,000 US military personnel in  the country so down significantly in  that period there has been criticism of  this agreement there are Republicans who  are worried they believe that the  Taliban have made promises in the past  and simply not delivered I think Mike  Pompeo was speaking directly to them in  his news conference in Doha just in the  last 20 minutes or so when he said we  know exactly who we are dealing with so  finally this administration seems to  have got a deal over the line you'll  remember that back in September there  was the possibility that the Taliban was  going to go to Camp David the  presidential retreat in Maryland that  was around the weekend of the 9/11  anniversary many people thought that was  incredibly bad timing torn deaf by the  Trump administration that perhaps played  a part and the rule in Donald Trump  suddenly canceling the talks although  there was the death of an American  soldier in the country at that time and  he said that the deal could be done he  might never do a deal with the Taliban  but we know behind the scenes in Doha  with the State Department with the envoi  of those discussions continued and that  took us to the point where we are today  for the motel and thank you of course I  will come back to you for more reaction  from the u.s. in Afghanistan of course  President Ashraf Ghani welcomed the  possible end of the war but also  cautioned that discussions between the  Taliban and his government what ongoing  our agenda for  discussion with the Taliban will be  wider than their discussions with the  United States they should be ready to  discuss their relationship whether the  state and non-state sponsors a  verifiable system for cutting off their  ties with all transnational terrorist  groups their dependents an involvement  with narcotics and other transnational  criminal organizations positively Bob  Bashara is our senior political analyst  here at al-jazeera Mar will be speaking  for the last couple of hours about  what's just gone on here in Doha yet in  Afghanistan words of caution real angst  amongst the public because at the end of  the day it's their lives that have been  in turmoil right at the grassroots at  the coalface so to speak for the last  four decades and they haven't got the  institutions they haven't got the real  politicians that they can have belief in  that can move this forward so it's is it  no wonder that everybody's asking will  this work it's it's the first step in a  thousand-mile was it were you know it's  very similar to Iraq but lacks a lot of  the institution that here Iraq had had  for long and Afghanistan has been going  through this for now for 40 years since  the Soviet invasion in 1979 until today  Afghanistan has been in a state of war  now regardless how you point a finger at  whom whether it is Washington's fault or  goddess fault  Moscow's fault Pakistan's fall Taliban  or whoever  Afghanistan has suffered for 40 years  what and while Matt Pompeo speaks of  nine thousand nine million students  going school in Afghanistan and that is  a good thing comparison to 2001 but 2001  was also after two decades of war so yes  there are some tangible achievements  within Kabul and within the centers of  population but the rest of Afghanistan  is divided among warlords and the  Taliban today so it's very hard to see  how this country is going to be able to  put together first of all it needs  healing  reconciliation then it needs to be  building  building not just State Building it's  actually now at this point in time  probably needs some nation-building as  well because the the the national fabric  has been torn apart so much for the last  40 years that to bring back trust among  say the Taliban and the Kabul government  it's gonna take along a long while and  between some of the liberal open-minded  elites or not so much elites just women  and men in Kabul who want full equality  who want social justice and the Taliban  that is insisting on some radical  phonetic interpretation of Islam that's  gonna take time so on all levels on the  social and religious on the economic on  the nation building on the state  building there's gonna be a lot of work  so just focus on one aspect of that and  at the moment we have a government in  Kabul you might say in crisis we have  the president and the CEO not speaking  to each other after and yet another  election and nobody really understanding  who's going to lead the negotiations if  they ever do get to the table with the  Taliban because that's what the US and  other stakeholders would like to see as  the next move within this next 135 days  my sense is that today's ceremony in  Kabul and I could be wrong that the  United States is interested to make sure  Ashraf Ghani remains the at the  forefront of this endeavor if you were  and his release of his own peace plan  back in October underlines this idea  that he's in sync with Washington over  what's next for Afghanistan because  while this thing is going to be going on  the unit's it's is going to remain  involve the next 14 months and I think  the United States will have a say over  who's who in Kabul and who will be  sitting with the Taliban because they  made that contingent over their  withdrawal from Afghanistan but really  it's going to be important for Afghan is  now to step up because the United States  for all practical purpose whether it's  the public or Washington they have  checked out in fact they probably have  concluded some 10 years ago after the  killing of Osama bin Laden that it was  time for them to leave and why haven't  they left  left well it's politics and the usual  Pride and Prejudice right but now  leaving they are and hence in Kabul as  well as the Taliban they need to pick up  the pieces come together because there's  going to be no one else other than them  that will be able to take Afghanistan to  the next step towards some sort of  national reconciliation who should see  what happens the rabbit Mohan thank you

